Rename the app to hitnrun, switch the accent to sky blue

Naming
- Every reference to the product this was modelled on is gone from code and
  comments; two remaining notes describe the behaviour without naming anyone.
- App identity is now hitnrun throughout: package name, productName, appId,
  window title, brand mark, User-Agent, control-server banner, MCP server name,
  and the control token header.
- Environment variables carried the old name too, so API_CLIENT_PORT/TOKEN/LABEL
  are now HITNRUN_PORT/TOKEN/LABEL.
- The project directory itself was renamed, which required re-registering the
  MCP server at its new path.

Colour
- Sky blue replaces the orange accent. Two shades on purpose: --accent (#38bdf8)
  is bright enough to read as text and borders on the dark background, but white
  text on it would fail contrast, so filled controls use --accent-solid
  (#0284c7). The light theme darkens --accent itself, since the bright shade is
  too pale on white.

Data migration
- Electron derives userData from productName, so the rename moved the data
  directory and stranded everything saved under the old name. On startup, a
  build with no workspace of its own now adopts a known previous product
  directory. It copies rather than moves, and only runs when there is nothing
  to overwrite, so it cannot clobber newer data.

Verified: 97 tests pass, the MCP server reconnects at the new path, and a
workspace saved under the old name is recovered on first launch.

